==============================================================================
N-011  dbscan_1d_optimal expands from NON-core members (border chaining)
==============================================================================
  eps=1.0 min_pts=3  prices=[100.0, 100.5, 101.0, 1.9, 2.4, 2.9, 3.4, 3.9]
    cluster -> [1.9, 2.4, 2.9, 3.4, 3.9]
    cluster -> [100.0, 100.5, 101.0]
  true DBSCAN core points (>= 3 neighbours incl. self): [100.0, 100.5, 101.0, 1.9, 2.4, 2.9, 3.4, 3.9]
  -> queue is seeded with the window of the FIRST core point but the
     inner l2/r2 expansion uses sorted_levels[idx] for EVERY dequeued
     member, so a border point drags in its own neighbours (border
     chaining), which standard DBSCAN forbids.

==============================================================================
N-012  dbscan_1d_optimal is NOT O(n log n) — timing on 300 / 3000 levels
==============================================================================
  n=  300 eps=0.80 min_pts=3 ->    1 clusters      0.27 ms
  n= 3000 eps=0.80 min_pts=3 ->    1 clusters      2.67 ms
  n= 9000 eps=0.80 min_pts=3 ->    1 clusters      8.49 ms

   ncalls  tottime  percall  cumtime  percall filename:lineno(function)
        1    0.020    0.020    0.027    0.027 /home/user/Upstage/apex/engines/e02_liquidity/engine.py:209(dbscan_1d_optimal)
    26998    0.001    0.000    0.001    0.000 {method 'append' of 'list' objects}
    18000    0.001    0.000    0.001    0.000 {method 'pop' of 'list' objects}
        1    0.001    0.001    0.002    0.002 {built-in method builtins.sorted}
        1    0.001    0.001    0.001    0.001 /home/user/Upstage/apex/engines/e02_liquidity/engine.py:254(<dictcomp>)
     9000    0.001    0.000    0.001    0.000 /home/user/Upstage/apex/engines/e02_liquidity/engine.py:217(<lambda>)


  per-cluster the seed scan re-walks `left` from 0 and re-derives the
  window; the inner BFS re-derives l2/r2 per member.  Complexity is
  O(n * cluster_reach) in practice, not O(n log n) as the docstring says.

==============================================================================
N-013  _update_fates caches the nearest HTF level per SIDE, not per level
==============================================================================
    level  102.00  salience=0.685604
    level  104.00  salience=0.685877
    level  128.00  salience=0.785598
    level  150.00  salience=0.687797
  proximity_htf(150.0, nearest=130.0) = 0.018316
  proximity_htf(102.0, nearest=100.0) = 0.670320
  -> p_htf_nearest_cache is keyed by lv.side, so the FIRST level of a
     side in dict order fixes the HTF reference price for every other
     level of that side in the same bar.

==============================================================================
N-014  a raid keeps re-emitting on later bars with no fresh sweep
==============================================================================
  sweeps=7 bars=[(13, 'lvl_108.36_12'), (24, 'lvl_112.16_23'), (26, 'lvl_111.96_19'), (36, 'lvl_113.96_24'), (46, 'lvl_115.96_34'), (49, 'lvl_111.84_47'), (56, 'lvl_117.96_44')]
  EV_LIQ_008 raid events = 1
    raid at_bar=49 side=SELL_SIDE count=2
  -> the raid window is `c.bar_index - s['at_bar'] <= raid_window` over
     sweep_log, which never expires, so the same 2 old sweeps re-qualify
     on every bar until a third sweep enters the window.
